        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>A new graphics card usually promises a boost in gaming performance. However, you don\u2019t always need a top-tier GPU to get the job done. If you don\u2019t typically game with the graphics cranked up or ray tracing enabled, you can actually get by with a significantly cheaper GPU. </p>\n\n\n\n<p>Before picking out a GPU, you need to figure out your preferred resolution. If you typically play at Full HD or WQHD resolution, I\u2019d recommend picking up a mid-range graphics card. According to Steam\u2019s <a href=\"https://store.steampowered.com/hwsurvey/Steam-Hardware-Software-Survey-Welcome-to-Steam\">Hardware Survey</a>, gaming at 1080p or 1440p resolution is the norm, while 4K gaming is less common.</p>\n\n\n<div class=\"extendedBlock-wrapper block-coreImage undefined\"><figure data-wp-context='{\"imageId\":\"6a6767367d557\"}' data-wp-interactive=\"core/image\" class=\"wp-block-image size-l",
